# AGENTS.md

{{synopsis}}. The owner reviews every change.
Write code that {{reader}} can read. Setup is in `README.md`.

## Checks

`scripts/check` runs every gate: gofumpt and import order, tidy `go.mod` and `go.sum`,
golangci-lint (which includes `go vet`), a search for files no check sees and for switched-off
checks, the build, and the tests with the race detector. The git hook and CI run it too.

- Work in small steps and keep `scripts/check` green after each one.
- Done means `scripts/check` is green. Show the command and the end of its output.
- When a check stays red after two honest attempts, stop and report what you tried.
- Fix the cause of a red check. Gate files change only with the owner's explicit approval:
  `scripts/check`, `.githooks/`, `.github/`, `.golangci.yml`, `mise.toml`, `mise.lock`,
  `.claude/`, the `go` line of `go.mod`, and existing tests. Never add another mise config
  file (`mise.local.toml`, `.tool-versions`, `.config/mise/`): `scripts/check` rejects them.

```sh
go test -race -count=1 -run '^TestGreetContainsName$' .   # one test
RAPID_SEED=1 go test -count=1 -run '^TestName$' .          # the inputs scripts/check uses
golangci-lint fmt                                          # format
golangci-lint run --fix                                    # lint autofixes; review the diff
```

`mise.toml` pins Go and golangci-lint. `scripts/check` runs under them by itself. In a shell
without mise, run the other commands as `mise exec -- <command>`.

Sandboxed agents: Go, golangci-lint and mise write to their caches (listed in
`.claude/settings.json`). A cold cache needs the network: `proxy.golang.org`, `sum.golang.org`,
and for `mise install` `dl.google.com` and `github.com`. `gh` needs the network too. Without
these, `scripts/check` fails for reasons outside the code.

## Go

Read `CODING_STANDARDS.md` before writing or reviewing Go.

The checks enforce: gofumpt formatting and import order, a doc comment on every declaration
(exported or not), handled errors (also `_ = f()` and `x.(T)`), wrapped errors from other
packages, no `reflect` outside tests and no `unsafe`, cgo, assembly or other non-Go files
without the owner's approval, no package-level variables or `init`, no test that skips itself
(`t.Skip` under any name, `testing.Short`, `testing.Verbose`, rapid's `t.Skip`), and no data
races. `//nolint`, `//go:norace`, other inline switches, `//line` directives and a
`Code generated` header do not switch a check off: fix the cause. A `TestMain` needs the
owner's approval (`allowed_testmain` in `scripts/check`), and it calls `m.Run()` and returns.
Test files may print only with `fmt.Print*`, which examples need for their `Output` comment.

Only a command, package main directly in `cmd/<name>/` (not its tests), may print, log through
the `log` or `slog` package functions, exit, panic, read the environment (`os.Getenv`) or the
command line (`os.Args`, `flag.Parse`) or use `syscall`, and only a command may have no tests.
The library logs on a `*slog.Logger` or `*log.Logger` it is given. No code asks whether it runs
under test (`testing.Testing`). Every other package, `cmd/<name>/internal/` included, runs at least one test.

Property tests use a fixed seed in `scripts/check`, so a run is repeatable. A plain `go test`
draws a random seed, so set `RAPID_SEED=1` and `-count=1` to rerun what the check ran. The
weekly `explore` workflow tries new seeds. When it fails, reproduce with the seed it prints.

Files hidden by a build tag or a platform file name (`_windows.go`) are rejected, because no
check would see them. That includes `//go:build race` and `!race`, since lint runs without the
race detector and the tests with it. A platform file needs the owner's approval: it goes into
`allowed_hidden` in `scripts/check`, with a CI job on its platform. Go files that `./...`
skips (folders named `testdata` or starting with `_` or `.`, nested modules) are rejected too,
except test fixtures under `testdata/` that no package imports.

`scripts/check` ignores settings saved with `go env -w`, and `GOPROXY`, `GOPRIVATE`, `CC` and
similar settings from the shell. Such settings go in the `[env]` table of `mise.toml`, with the
owner's approval.

## Layout

- Logic goes in the root package `{{gopackage}}`. `cmd/{{package}}/main.go` only calls it.
- New packages go under `internal/`, unless the owner wants them public.
- The module path is `{{module}}`. Change it with `mise exec -- go mod edit -module <path>`,
  and update the imports and this line, before anyone imports the module.
- To change Go, update `mise.toml` (`go = "X.Y.Z"`) and the `go` line of `go.mod` (`go X.Y.0`)
  together, then run `mise lock`. Bump golangci-lint in the same change: it must be built with
  Go X.Y or newer, where X.Y is the `go` line.

## Writing

For chat replies, commits, PRs, code comments and docs. Many readers are not native English
speakers.

- Short sentences and plain words. Lead with the result. Then what the reader needs to act: what
  changed, what failed, what is left, what you did not verify.
- Say what a technical idea does before you name it.
- A chat reply fits one screen. Use a table for parallel facts and a numbered list for steps.
  Keep every risk and open question.
- Name files by repo-relative path.
- Docs state rules and pointers, never project status. Status goes stale.
- Commits and PRs describe the change only, with no tool or AI attribution.
- PR title: `type(scope): what changed`, at most 72 characters. CI checks it. PR body: fill
  `.github/pull_request_template.md`, one line per slot. One concern per PR, about 200 changed
  lines at most. Open it with `gh pr create --assignee @me`, so the owner is assigned. Stack
  bigger work as PRs whose base is the branch below. Squash merges make the PR title and body the
  commit on `main`.

## Review

Review each PR on its own. Before a stack merges, review it once as a whole for problems between
PRs only:

```sh
git diff $(git merge-base origin/main <top>)..<top>
```
